    .timeline-row--bad span:last-child { color: var(--danger); }
  </style>

  <h2>The Problem</h2>

  <p>
    This is the standard way to add a background video loop to a page: a promo tile in a product grid, a hero
    banner, an ambient clip. The video is muted so it may autoplay, and inline so iOS doesn't open the
    full-screen player. <code>preload="none"</code> stops it downloading until it's needed, and an
    <strong>IntersectionObserver</strong> starts it when it scrolls into view.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="html"
    filename="index.html"
    code={`<video class="lazy-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster="poster.jpg">
  <source src="clip.mp4" type="video/mp4">
</video>`}
  />

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="lazy-video.js"
    code={`const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  entries.forEach((entry) => {
    const video = entry.target;
    if (entry.isIntersecting && video.paused) {
      video.play().catch(() => {});
    }
  });
});

document.querySelectorAll('.lazy-video').forEach((video) => observer.observe(video));`}
  />

  <p>What you see:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chrome:</strong> the video starts every time it scrolls into view.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safari (iPhone and Mac):</strong> it usually starts, but on some page loads the tile shows only
      the poster, forever. There is no console error, and the Network panel has no request for
      <code>clip.mp4</code>. Reloading often "fixes" it, so it tends to reach QA as "the video sometimes doesn't show".
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="mockup-row">
    <IphoneMockup label="Safari: stuck on the poster">
      <div class="grid-screen">
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile tile--video">
          <span class="live-dot live-dot--stuck">POSTER ONLY</span>
          <div class="tile-debug">
            paused: <span class="bad">false</span><br />
            readyState: <span class="bad">0</span> (HAVE_NOTHING)<br />
            requests: <span class="bad">none</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
      </div>
    </IphoneMockup>

    <IphoneMockup label="Chrome, or Safari after the fix">
      <div class="grid-screen">
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile tile--video tile--playing">
          <span class="live-dot">PLAYING</span>
          <div class="tile-debug">
            paused: <span class="good">false</span><br />
            readyState: <span class="good">4</span> (HAVE_ENOUGH_DATA)<br />
            requests: <span class="good">clip.mp4 206</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
        <div class="tile"></div>
      </div>
    </IphoneMockup>
  </div>

  <p>
    It happens most when the <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> has <strong>just been inserted</strong> and is already in
    view. Typical cases are a list rendered client-side (React, Preact, Vue), a grid swapped through
    <code>innerHTML</code> after filtering or sorting, or a video near the top of the page. You can confirm a
    stuck tile in the console:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="console"
    code={`[...document.querySelectorAll('video')].map((v) => ({
  paused: v.paused,             // false…
  readyState: v.readyState,     // …but 0: no data at all
  networkState: v.networkState, // 1: idle / suspended
  preload: v.preload,           // "none"
}));`}
  />

  <p>
    The tell is <code>paused: false</code> together with <code>readyState: 0</code>. The video thinks it is
    playing, but it has no data and isn't fetching any.
  </p>

  <h2>Root Cause</h2>

  <h3>How a &lt;video&gt; loads</h3>

  <p>
    A video element is a small state machine, not an image that moves. These are the steps between inserting
    the element and seeing motion:
  </p>

  <ol class="pipeline">
    <li>
      <span class="step-title">Element created</span>
      Parsed, set through <code>innerHTML</code>, or inserted by a framework. Nothing is chosen or fetched yet.
      <span class="step-meta">networkState 0 · readyState 0</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step-title">Resource selection (asynchronous)</span>
      The browser walks the <code>&lt;source&gt;</code> children in order and takes the <strong>first</strong> one it
      might be able to play. It doesn't look for the best one.
      <span class="step-meta">fires loadstart · currentSrc set</span>
    </li>
    <li class="step--gate">
      <span class="step-title">The preload gate</span>
      With <code>none</code> the load is suspended here, and nothing is fetched until playback is requested.
      <code>metadata</code> and <code>auto</code> carry on fetching.
      <span class="step-meta">networkState 1 (suspended) or 2 (loading)</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step-title">Fetch and decode</span>
      Byte-range requests for MP4, or a playlist and its segments for HLS. <code>readyState</code> climbs.
      <span class="step-meta">loadedmetadata → loadeddata → canplay → canplaythrough</span>
    </li>
    <li class="step--gate">
      <span class="step-title">play() and the autoplay policy</span>
      A muted, inline video may start without a user gesture unless the platform blocks it, for example in Low
      Power Mode. A blocked start rejects with <code>NotAllowedError</code>.
      <span class="step-meta">fires play · paused becomes false straight away</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step-title">First frame painted</span>
      Only now are frames on screen.
      <span class="step-meta">fires playing · the play() promise resolves</span>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Note that <code>paused</code> flips to <code>false</code> at step 5, before any data exists. Only the
    <code>playing</code> event means real frames are showing. With <code>preload="none"</code>, the element sits at
    step 3 until <code>play()</code> wakes it up.
  </p>

  <h3>The race</h3>

  <p>
    <code>play()</code> is supposed to wake a load suspended at step 3, and in Chrome it always does. In WebKit,
    the result depends on <em>when</em> you call it. If <code>play()</code> arrives <strong>before resource
    selection has run</strong>, WebKit records the play request and marks the element unpaused. Resource
    selection then reaches <code>preload="none"</code> and suspends, and nothing ever wakes it. A logged stuck
    load in WebKit (timings rounded):
  </p>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="timeline-row"><span>0 ms</span><span><code>&lt;video preload="none"&gt;</code> inserted</span></div>
    <div class="timeline-row"><span>+2 ms</span><span>observer fires immediately (already in view) → <code>play()</code></span></div>
    <div class="timeline-row"><span>+3 ms</span><span><code>play</code>, <code>waiting</code>: paused is now false</span></div>
    <div class="timeline-row"><span>+5 ms</span><span><code>loadstart</code>: resource selection only now picks a source</span></div>
    <div class="timeline-row timeline-row--bad"><span>∞</span><span>suspended by preload="none": no request, and play() never settles</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    When <code>play()</code> comes <em>after</em> <code>loadstart</code>, the load resumes normally. That is why the bug
    is intermittent. It depends on whether the observer's first callback lands before or after resource
    selection. In our headless WebKit runs, a freshly inserted, already-visible video got stuck on every
    cold load. Chrome never did.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Why it never recovers:</strong> the stuck video reports <code>paused === false</code>, so the
    <code>if (video.paused)</code> guard in the observer skips it. Scrolling away and back does nothing.
  </div>

  <h3>Other differences that look similar</h3>

  <p>Several other Chrome/Safari differences produce a "video didn't show" report:</p>

  <div class="compare-wrap">
    <table class="compare">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Behaviour</th><th>Chrome (Blink)</th><th>Safari (WebKit, iOS &amp; macOS)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>preload="none"</code> then <code>play()</code></td>
          <td>Always resumes the load</td>
          <td class="diff">An early <code>play()</code> can leave it suspended forever (this article)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Muted autoplay</td>
          <td>Allowed</td>
          <td class="diff">Blocked in Low Power Mode or when the site is set to "Never Auto-Play": <code>NotAllowedError</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Between <code>play()</code> and the first frame</td>
          <td>Keeps showing the poster</td>
          <td class="diff">Can paint the element blank even with <code>poster</code> set</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Native HLS (<code>.m3u8</code>)</td>
          <td>Recent versions report support too (we saw <code>canPlayType</code> → "maybe" in Chrome 154)</td>
          <td>Supported. An HLS source listed first is chosen, and it starts at a low rendition</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>MP4 delivery</td>
          <td>Tolerates servers without range support</td>
          <td>Needs byte-range (<code>206</code>) responses</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The quickest way to tell them apart is the state of the stuck element:</p>

  <div class="compare-wrap">
    <table class="compare">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Cause</th><th>play() promise</th><th>paused</th><th>readyState</th><th>Network</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>This race</td><td>never settles</td><td class="diff">false</td><td class="diff">0</td><td class="diff">no request</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Autoplay policy</td><td>rejects <code>NotAllowedError</code></td><td>true</td><td>any</td><td>may have fetched</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Broken file / CORS / codec</td><td>rejects, or an <code>error</code> event fires</td><td>varies</td><td>0</td><td>request fails</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>On iOS, every browser is WebKit.</strong> Chrome on iPhone behaves like the Safari column.
    "Works in Chrome" on a laptop says nothing about Chrome on an iPhone.
  </div>

  <h2>The Fix</h2>

  <h3>Step 1: Lift preload right before play()</h3>

  <p>
    This is the core fix, and it is one line. On the element you are about to play, set <code>preload</code> to
    <code>auto</code> immediately before <code>play()</code>. Whichever order WebKit runs things in, resource
    selection then finds nothing telling it to suspend.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="lazy-video.js"
    code={`function playVideo(video) {
  video.preload = 'auto'; // don't let preload="none" win the race against play()
  return video.play();
}`}
  />

  <p>
    You keep the bandwidth saving. Only a video that actually reaches <code>playVideo()</code> loses
    <code>preload="none"</code>. Off-screen videos, and copies hidden with <code>display: none</code> (which
    never intersect), still download nothing.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Add a one-shot watchdog</h3>

  <p>
    A video can stall in other ways too, such as a dropped first request or a CDN hiccup. And a stalled video
    never retries, because it already reports unpaused. If no data has arrived a few seconds after
    <code>play()</code>, reload it once:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="lazy-video.js"
    code={`function watchStart(video) {
  if (video._startTimer || video._restarted) return; // one watchdog, one retry

  video._startTimer = setTimeout(() => {
    video._startTimer = null;
    if (!document.contains(video) || video.readyState > 0 || video.classList.contains('is-playing')) return;

    video._restarted = true;
    video.load(); // re-runs resource selection; preload is 'auto' by now
    video.play()?.catch(() => {});
  }, 4000);
}`}
  />

  <h3>Step 3: Don't swallow autoplay rejections</h3>

  <p>
    <code>.catch(() =&gt; {'{}'})</code> hides the Low Power Mode case, so the tile never starts and nothing says
    why. Reloading won't help either, because Safari wants a gesture. Remember the blocked videos and retry
    them on the next tap, click or key press:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="lazy-video.js"
    code={`const blocked = new Set();
const GESTURES = ['touchend', 'click', 'keydown'];

function retryBlocked() {
  GESTURES.forEach((type) => document.removeEventListener(type, retryBlocked, true));
  blocked.forEach((video) => { if (document.contains(video)) playVideo(video); });
  blocked.clear();
}

function onPlayRejected(video, error) {
  if (error?.name !== 'NotAllowedError') return;
  if (!blocked.size) GESTURES.forEach((type) => document.addEventListener(type, retryBlocked, true));
  blocked.add(video);
}`}
  />

  <h3>Step 4: Never show a blank tile</h3>

  <p>
    Safari can paint the video blank between <code>play()</code> and the first frame. Put the poster on the
    container's background as well, and keep the video transparent until <code>playing</code> fires. A slow
    or failed start then looks like a still image.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="html"
    filename="index.html"
    code={`<div class="video-tile" style="background-image: url('poster.jpg')">
  <video class="lazy-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster="poster.jpg">
    <source src="clip.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  </video>
</div>`}
  />

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    filename="video-tile.css"
    code={`.video-tile {
  position: relative;
  background-position: center;
  background-size: cover;
}

.lazy-video {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s;
}

.lazy-video.is-playing {
  opacity: 1;
}`}
  />

  <h3>Step 5: All together</h3>

  <p>
    Call <code>observeVideos()</code> once on load, and again after anything re-renders the list. The
    <code>data-lazy-observed</code> guard makes repeat calls safe.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="lazy-video.js"
    code={`const LazyVideo = {
  _observer: null,
  _blocked: new Set(),

  _play(video) {
    video.preload = 'auto';                 // Step 1
    const playPromise = video.play();
    this._watchStart(video);                // Step 2
    playPromise?.catch((error) => {         // Step 3
      if (error?.name !== 'NotAllowedError') return;
      if (!this._blocked.size) {
        const retry = () => {
          ['touchend', 'click', 'keydown'].forEach((type) => document.removeEventListener(type, retry, true));
          this._blocked.forEach((v) => { if (document.contains(v)) this._play(v); });
          this._blocked.clear();
        };
        ['touchend', 'click', 'keydown'].forEach((type) => document.addEventListener(type, retry, true));
      }
      this._blocked.add(video);
    });
  },

  _watchStart(video) {
    if (video._startTimer || video._restarted) return;
    video._startTimer = setTimeout(() => {
      video._startTimer = null;
      if (!document.contains(video) || video.readyState > 0 || video.classList.contains('is-playing')) return;
      video._restarted = true;
      video.load();
      video.play()?.catch(() => {});
    }, 4000);
  },

  _getObserver() {
    this._observer ??= new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      entries.forEach((entry) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting && entry.target.paused) this._play(entry.target);
      });
    }, { rootMargin: '200px 0px' }); // start just before it scrolls on screen
    return this._observer;
  },

  observeVideos(root = document) {
    if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
    root.querySelectorAll('video.lazy-video:not([data-lazy-observed])').forEach((video) => {
      video.setAttribute('data-lazy-observed', 'true');
      video.addEventListener('playing', () => video.classList.add('is-playing')); // Step 4
      this._getObserver().observe(video);
    });
  },
};

LazyVideo.observeVideos();
// After a client-side re-render: LazyVideo.observeVideos(container);`}
  />

  <h3>Verifying it</h3>

  <p>
    The bug is intermittent, so one manual reload proves nothing. Count successes over many cold loads in
    headless WebKit instead:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    filename="verify-video-start.mjs"
    code={`// npm i -D playwright && npx playwright install webkit
import { webkit } from 'playwright';

const browser = await webkit.launch();
let ok = 0;

for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  const context = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1440, height: 900 } }); // cold cache
  const page = await context.newPage();
  await page.goto(process.argv[2], { waitUntil: 'load' });
  await page.waitForTimeout(6000); // longer than the watchdog

  const playing = await page.$$eval('video.lazy-video', (videos) => videos
    .filter((v) => v.offsetParent !== null)
    .every((v) => !v.paused && v.readyState >= 3));
  if (playing) ok++;
  await context.close();
}

console.log(ok + '/10 cold loads playing');
await browser.close();`}
  />

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Harness gotcha:</strong> Playwright's bundled Chromium has no H.264, so an MP4 there reports
    <code>NETWORK_NO_SOURCE</code>. Use WebKit as the Safari proxy, check Chrome in real Chrome, and confirm on a
    real iPhone before shipping.
  </div>

  <h2>Applying It in Shopify (video_tag)</h2>

  <p>
    Shopify video (a <code>video</code> section setting, a metafield, or a metaobject field) is usually rendered
    with the <code>video_tag</code> filter. Everything above applies, but <code>video_tag</code> has two defaults
    worth knowing about.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="liquid"
    filename="sections/promo-video.liquid"
    code={`<div class="video-tile" style="background-image: url('{{ section.settings.video.preview_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}')">
  {{
    section.settings.video
    | video_tag:
      class: 'lazy-video',
      muted: true,
      loop: true,
      playsinline: true,
      preload: 'none'
  }}
</div>`}
  />

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Source order is Shopify's, not yours.</strong> <code>video_tag</code> emits one
      <code>&lt;source&gt;</code> per entry in <code>video.sources</code>, in stored order. That is usually the
      HLS <code>.m3u8</code> playlist first, then the MP4 renditions. Safari, and recent Chrome, take the HLS
      source, and adaptive streaming starts at a low rendition. On a large desktop tile, the first seconds look
      noticeably softer than the poster. That's fine for small or mobile tiles. For large ones, see below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't pass <code>autoplay: true</code> for below-the-fold video.</strong> It starts the download
      immediately, for every copy, including one hidden at another breakpoint. Use <code>preload: 'none'</code> and
      the observer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The poster is set for you</strong> from <code>video.preview_image</code>. Still mirror it as the
      container background (as above) for Step 4.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you need to control the order, for example the sharpest MP4 first on desktop, build the element
    yourself from <code>video.sources</code>:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="liquid"
    filename="snippets/lazy-video.liquid"
    code={`{%- assign hls_sources = video.sources | where: 'format', 'm3u8' -%}
{%- assign mp4_sources = video.sources | where: 'format', 'mp4' | sort: 'height' | reverse -%}
{%- assign ordered_sources = mp4_sources | concat: hls_sources -%}

<video
  class="lazy-video"
  muted
  loop
  playsinline
  preload="none"
  poster="{{ video.preview_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}"
>
  {%- for source in ordered_sources -%}
    <source src="{{ source.url }}" type="{{ source.mime_type }}">
  {%- endfor -%}
</video>`}
  />

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Two copies for two breakpoints?</strong> A common theme pattern renders a desktop and a mobile
    video and hides one with CSS. That is fine with <code>preload="none"</code> and the observer: the hidden copy
    never intersects, so it never loads. For the mobile copy, list HLS first (lighter on cellular); for desktop,
    MP4 first.
  </div>

  <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

  <div class="compare-wrap">
    <table class="compare">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What you see</th><th>Do this instead</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Calling <code>play()</code> on a fresh <code>preload="none"</code> video</td>
          <td>Safari: stuck on the poster, intermittently</td>
          <td>Set <code>preload = 'auto'</code> first (Step 1)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Treating <code>!paused</code> as success</td>
          <td>Stalled videos are never retried</td>
          <td>Use <code>playing</code> / <code>readyState</code>, plus a watchdog</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>play().catch(() =&gt; {'{}'})</code></td>
          <td>Low Power Mode: never starts, no clue why</td>
          <td>Handle <code>NotAllowedError</code> and retry on a gesture</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Poster attribute only</td>
          <td>Blank tile in Safari while starting, or forever if it fails</td>
          <td>Poster as container background, and fade in on <code>playing</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Accepting the default source order</td>
          <td>HLS chosen; large tiles look soft</td>
          <td>Order sources on purpose per breakpoint</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>autoplay</code> or <code>preload="auto"</code> on every copy</td>
          <td>Hidden and off-screen videos download too</td>
          <td><code>preload="none"</code> + an observer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Waiting on an app event or timer before observing</td>
          <td>Visible delay before motion</td>
          <td>Observe immediately, and re-observe after re-renders</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

